<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Groningen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Groningen is the main city of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and capital of the province of the same name. A trading town and member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it long dominated the surrounding Frisian countryside, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ommelanden">Ommelanden</a>. It came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule in 1536, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> after its capture by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange">Maurice of Nassau</a> in 1594, and its university was founded in 1614.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/groningen.jpg" alt="Groningen" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JAHensens · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoge_der_A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Groningen lies at the northernmost point of the Hondsrug area. The origin of its name is uncertain: a folk story tells of exiles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> led by a figure called Gruno, one modern theory takes it to mean among the people of Groni, from an old Frisian personal name, and another derives it from a word for green fields. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sometimes call it Martinistad, after the Martinitoren tower.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest document referring to the city dates from 1040, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> had occupied the area centuries earlier. It gained city rights de facto in 1245, but no written record of a formal grant survives. Isolated from the successive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> centres of power in Utrecht, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, it long relied on itself and nearby regions. As a Hanseatic city it belonged to the North German trade network and later became chiefly a regional market centre. In the 13th century its people built a city wall, and the Gronings dialect became common in the surrounding lands.</p><p>Its most influential period came at the end of the 15th century, when the province of Friesland was administered from Groningen and the Martinitoren was built. At the height of its power it could be considered an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>. In 1536 it accepted Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> as its ruler, which ended its autonomy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange">Maurice of Nassau</a> captured it in the Siege of Groningen of 1594, after which the city and the province joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. The University of Groningen was founded in 1614, and the city grew rapidly, with a new wall.</p><p>In the Siege of Groningen of 1672, during the Third Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Dutch War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Münster, Bernhard von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a>, failed to take the city, an event still marked on 28 August as Gronings Ontzet. In 1811 Napoleon I annexed it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>, until 1813. In April 1945, in the Battle of Groningen, the main square and the Grote Markt were largely destroyed, though the Martinitoren, the Goudkantoor and the city hall were undamaged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Groningen is the capital of its province and the largest city of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, with 244,807 inhabitants. The University of Groningen is the second oldest university in the country, and students make up about a quarter of the population.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Groningen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Leicester left in 1587 the States General stopped seeking a monarch and governed as a republic of seven provinces, Holland, Zeeland, Utrecht, Gelderland, Overijssel, Friesland and <b>Groningen</b>, with conquered lands to the south ruled in common as the Generality Lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1945 the country built a generous welfare state, beginning with the state old-age pension of 1957, and the discovery of the <b>Groningen</b> natural gas field in 1959 brought great revenues.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Carbon-14 dates from the University of <b>Groningen</b> and pottery give a sequence of crafts from the 10th century BCE: melting pots, casting nozzles, weights, worked wood, ship parts, ivory from the only workshop of its time proven in the west, gold and silver.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tartessos">Tartessos · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish musicians played it mockingly at the siege of Alkmaar in 1573; it was played at William's entry into Brussels in 1577 and after the liberation of <b>Groningen</b> in 1594.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wilhelmus">Wilhelmus</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles V, born at Ghent in 1500, rounded off the territories by taking Frisia, Utrecht, Overijssel, <b>Groningen</b> and Guelders, forming the Seventeen Provinces.</blockquote><a href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a></li><li><blockquote>Saxon troops imposed their rule with difficulty against Frisian resistance and the city of <b>Groningen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-frisia">Lordship of Frisia</a></li></ul></section>
